title = GitLab.com vs self-managed: what moves where
mode = platform_theory

[diagram]

[center][color=#fca326]SaaS[/color]  vs  [color=#6b4fbb]self-managed[/color][/center]

[code]
   gitlab.com                    your VPC / airgap
   ┌──────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ shared runners   │         │ your runners + LDAP/SAML │
   │ upgrades by GL   │         │ you patch + scale        │
   │ storage quotas   │         │ object storage backends  │
   └──────────────────┘         └──────────────────────────┘
           │                                  │
           └──────── same Git push URL ───────┘
[/code]

[center][b]Git remote URL[/b] still points at GitLab HTTP/SSH endpoint[/center]

[code]
   # Git commands identical; ops boundary differs
   git push origin main
[/code]

[description]

[b]GitLab.com[/b] hosts projects on GitLab infrastructure: shared runners (with minute quotas on free tiers), managed upgrades, and tiered compliance features (audit events, geo replication on higher plans). Onboarding is fast; you accept vendor-operated boundaries on runner isolation, storage quotas, and support SLAs.

[b]Self-managed deployments:[/b] Omnibus packages or Helm charts run Rails, Gitaly, PostgreSQL, Redis, and supporting services in your data center or cloud VPC. You attach identity providers (LDAP, SAML, OIDC), configure object storage for artifacts and LFS, and register runners on VMs or Kubernetes executors meeting compliance. Upgrades, backups, and capacity become your operational work—plan maintenance windows like any critical app.

[b]What stays the same:[/b] Git protocol, branch semantics, merge strategies, and local workflows from Oh My Git—clone, branch, rebase, push. Developers often cannot tell SaaS vs self-managed from Git alone; differences appear in SSO login URLs, runner labels, and feature flags.

[b]Hybrid patterns:[/b] some orgs use GitLab.com for OSS mirrors while proprietary code stays self-managed, or replicate between them with push mirrors. Document canonical origin to avoid double MR triage. Licensing tiers unlock merge trains, security policies, compliance dashboards—Git layer ignores license; humans and license files do not.

[b]Runner placement:[/b] SaaS shared runners convenient; regulated workloads demand private runners with network egress controls. Register runners at instance, group, or project scope; protect registration tokens like production secrets. Match [code]tags:[/code] in YAML to runner capabilities ([code]docker[/code], [code]shell[/code], [code]k8s[/code]) or jobs wait indefinitely.

[b]Disaster recovery:[/b] GitLab is database plus object storage—protect PostgreSQL, Redis, Gitaly repo storage, CI artifact buckets, and container registry. Practice restore drills; external Git mirrors alone do not restore issues, MR history, or pipeline logs.

[b]Airgap updates:[/b] self-managed airgap installs import offline update packages—plan cadence lest security patches lag. Runner images in disconnected environments need internal mirrors of Docker bases referenced in [code].gitlab-ci.yml[/code].

[b]Storage tiering:[/b] SaaS storage quotas encourage LFS and artifact cleanup policies; self-managed operators attach S3-compatible object storage for Gitaly secondary storage—misconfigured buckets show up as intermittent clone failures under load, not obvious permission errors.

[b]When things go wrong:[/b] “This feature unavailable” on self-managed often means license tier or incomplete upgrade—check Administration. Geo-replication lag causes MR merge conflicts across sites—verify replication health. SaaS rate limits on API during bulk imports look like flakiness—backoff and retry with idempotency keys. Backup restore missing CI history means artifact bucket restore omitted—verify object storage lifecycle rules.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] pick SaaS when you want the vendor to run the platform; pick self-managed when policy demands data and runners stay inside your fence. Git commands stay the same either way.
